---
import SidebarLayout from '../../layouts/SidebarLayout.astro';
---
<SidebarLayout title="FAQ">
  <header>
    <div data-slot="title">
      <div data-slot="content">
        <h1>FAQ</h1>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div data-slot="description">

      Searchable knowledge base with categorized collapsible sections.
      Demonstrates <code>n-accordion</code> with live filtering via <code>n-input</code>.

    </div>
  </header>
  <main>
<n-stack gap="6">

    <div class="faq-wrapper">
      <!-- ── Search ── -->
      <div class="faq-search">
        <n-input placeholder="Search questions..." id="faq-search">
          <n-icon name="magnifying-glass" slot="leading"></n-icon>
        </n-input>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Getting Started ── -->
      <div class="faq-category" data-category>
        <h2 class="faq-category-title">
          <n-icon name="rocket-launch"></n-icon>
          Getting Started
        </h2>
        <n-accordion>
          <n-accordion-item open data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">How do I install native-ui?</span>
            <p>Install via npm with <code>npm install @nonoun/native-ui</code>. Then import the CSS foundation and register the components in your app entry point. See the quick start guide for framework-specific setup instructions.</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
          <n-accordion-item data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">Does native-ui work with React, Vue, or Svelte?</span>
            <p>Yes. Native-ui components are standard web components built on the Custom Elements API. They work in any framework that renders to the DOM. No wrapper libraries or adapters needed.</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
          <n-accordion-item data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">What browsers are supported?</span>
            <p>All modern evergreen browsers: Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge. The library uses standard web platform APIs (Custom Elements, CSS custom properties, Popover API) and does not require polyfills in supported browsers.</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
        </n-accordion>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Styling ── -->
      <div class="faq-category" data-category>
        <h2 class="faq-category-title">
          <n-icon name="sparkle"></n-icon>
          Styling &amp; Theming
        </h2>
        <n-accordion>
          <n-accordion-item data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">How do I customize the color theme?</span>
            <p>Override the OKLCH design tokens on your root element. The entire color system is derived from a single hue value, so changing <code>--n-hue-accent</code> recolors every accent-tinted component automatically. Dark mode is handled via <code>color-scheme</code> and requires no additional tokens.</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
          <n-accordion-item data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">Why doesn't shadow DOM scoping work?</span>
            <p>Native-ui uses light DOM exclusively. This means you style components with plain CSS selectors — no <code>::part()</code> or <code>::slotted()</code> needed. The tradeoff is that component styles aren't encapsulated, but the benefit is full control over every visual detail.</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
          <n-accordion-item data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">Can I use Tailwind CSS alongside native-ui?</span>
            <p>Absolutely. Since native-ui components are light DOM and use CSS custom properties, Tailwind utility classes work directly on component elements. You can mix design token values and Tailwind classes freely.</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
        </n-accordion>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Components ── -->
      <div class="faq-category" data-category>
        <h2 class="faq-category-title">
          <n-icon name="cube"></n-icon>
          Components
        </h2>
        <n-accordion>
          <n-accordion-item data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">How do I handle form validation?</span>
            <p>Use the <code>required</code>, <code>pattern</code>, and <code>minlength</code> attributes on <code>n-field</code> wrappers. The validation trait handles constraint checking, error display, and form-level reset automatically. No JavaScript needed for basic validation.</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
          <n-accordion-item data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">What's the difference between n-dialog and overlays?</span>
            <p><code>n-dialog</code> is a native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> wrapper with <code>showModal()</code> support, backdrop, and focus trapping. Overlays (via <code>PresentController</code>) are programmatic and return a <code>closed</code> promise, making them ideal for confirmation flows and transient UI.</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
          <n-accordion-item data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">How do trait controllers work?</span>
            <p>Traits are composable behaviors attached via the <code>traits</code> attribute on <code>n-controller</code>. Examples include <code>draggable</code>, <code>sortable</code>, <code>copyable</code>, and <code>validatable</code>. Each trait fires custom events (<code>native:*</code>) and sets runtime attributes for styling. Consumers handle the logic; the library manages the interaction.</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
        </n-accordion>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Troubleshooting ── -->
      <div class="faq-category" data-category>
        <h2 class="faq-category-title">
          <n-icon name="warning"></n-icon>
          Troubleshooting
        </h2>
        <n-accordion>
          <n-accordion-item data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">Components render but have no styling</span>
            <p>CSS is not bundled with JavaScript. You must import the foundation stylesheet separately: <code>@import '@nonoun/native-ui/css/foundation'</code>. In Astro, use <code>&lt;style is:global&gt;</code> with <code>@import</code> — never <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> for npm packages.</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
          <n-accordion-item data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">Icons are not rendering</span>
            <p>Icons must be explicitly registered before use. Import the SVG files with <code>?raw</code> and call <code>registerIcon(name, svg)</code> for each icon you need. The library does not bundle icon SVGs — this keeps the bundle small and gives you full control over which icons are included.</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
          <n-accordion-item data-faq>
            <span slot="heading">Server-side rendering throws HTMLElement errors</span>
            <p>Component classes extend <code>HTMLElement</code>, which doesn't exist in Node.js. Never import component classes on the server. Use dynamic imports or ensure registration code only runs in the browser (e.g., inside <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags in Astro).</p>
          </n-accordion-item>
        </n-accordion>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-no-results" id="faq-no-results" hidden>
        <n-icon name="magnifying-glass" size="lg" class="faq-no-results-icon"></n-icon>
        <p>No matching questions found.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </n-stack>
  </main>

  <script>
    document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
      const searchInput = document.getElementById('faq-search');
      if (!searchInput) return;
      const items = document.querySelectorAll('[data-faq]');
      const categories = document.querySelectorAll('[data-category]');
      const noResults = document.getElementById('faq-no-results');

      searchInput.addEventListener('input', () => {
        const query = (searchInput as HTMLInputElement).value.toLowerCase().trim();
        let visibleCount = 0;

        items.forEach((item) => {
          const el = item as HTMLElement;
          const heading = el.querySelector('[slot="heading"]')?.textContent?.toLowerCase() || '';
          const body = el.querySelector('p')?.textContent?.toLowerCase() || '';
          const matches = !query || heading.includes(query) || body.includes(query);
          el.style.display = matches ? '' : 'none';
          if (matches) visibleCount++;
        });

        // Show/hide entire categories based on whether they have visible items
        categories.forEach((cat) => {
          const catEl = cat as HTMLElement;
          const hasVisible = catEl.querySelector('[data-faq]:not([style*="display: none"])');
          catEl.style.display = hasVisible ? '' : 'none';
        });

        if (noResults) noResults.hidden = visibleCount > 0;
      });
    });
  </script>
  <style is:global>
    .faq-wrapper {
      max-width: 44rem;
    }

    .faq-search {
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    .faq-category {
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    .faq-category-title {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.5rem;
      font-size: 0.9375rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
      color: var(--n-ink-strong-neutral);
    }

    .faq-no-results {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: center;
      text-align: center;
      padding: 3rem 2rem;
    }

    .faq-no-results-icon {
      color: var(--n-ink-muted-neutral);
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }

    .faq-no-results p {
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      color: var(--n-ink-muted-neutral);
      margin: 0;
    }

    [data-faq] p {
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      color: var(--n-ink-muted-neutral);
      line-height: 1.6;
      margin: 0;
    }

    [data-faq] code {
      font-size: 0.8125rem;
      background: var(--n-panel-neutral);
      padding: 0.0625rem 0.375rem;
      border-radius: 0.25rem;
      font-family: ui-monospace, monospace;
    }
</style>

</SidebarLayout>
